Derived portions. Each was ported through Hitman's Halo, a private fork of
gods-eye-view, and follows gods-eye-view's implementation:

- workers/mccluster/src/world-halo-layers.js: the provider adapters for USGS
  earthquakes, NASA FIRMS active fires (the CSV read by header name; the
  unpadded acquisition time to UTC), NIFC WFIGS fire perimeters (the
  FeatureServer query and its field list), NOAA NHC current storms (id and
  basin handling; movement converted from miles per hour to knots), adsb.lol
  military aircraft, AIS "not available" values, and the Photon and Nominatim
  place search.
- workers/mccluster/src/world-layers.js: the flight, CelesTrak and Launch
  Library 2 adapters (the adsb.lol unit conversions and filtering, the
  CelesTrak groups, the Launch Library token header).
- js/halo/vision.js: the Halo vision post-process shaders, adapted through
  Hitman's Halo; its notice has also been kept at
  vendor/LICENSES/gods-eye-view-MIT.txt since it was added.

The environmental globe (slice G1, 2026-11-17) adapted three more portions
straight from gods-eye-view; each of these files carries a one-line pointer to
this file in its header:

- js/globe-render-governor.js: the render governor (src/renderGovernor.js: the
  owner-keyed holds, the camera hold from moveStart to moveEnd, the one-shot
  render requests and the diagnostics), adapted to Cesium's requestRenderMode
  on the World's globe.
- js/globe-scene-director.js: the scene director's camera flights
  (src/director/camera.js, the shortest-arc longitude interpolation), the
  ordered scene queue and its phases (src/director/playback.js) and the
  manual-input interrupt (src/cameraVerbs.js).
- js/environment-layers.js: the alpha-only pulse pattern
  (src/render/cesium/draw.js, pulseAlpha and runPulses) for the one labelled
  ripple on a selected earthquake; the geometry is never touched after it is
  built.

workers/mccluster/src/world-environment-layers.js calls helpers exported by
world-halo-layers.js; its own code is not derived from gods-eye-view.

